  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "@id": "https://steves-heating-and-air.vercel.app/services/ductwork/#faq",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Why is one room in my house always hotter or colder than the rest?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Uneven room temperatures are almost always a duct or airflow problem, not an equipment problem. The duct run to that room may be leaking, crushed, disconnected, too long, or undersized — so it never gets its share of conditioned air. Replacing the AC or furnace won&#x27;t fix it. We diagnose the airflow, find the actual cause, and fix that."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How do I know if my ducts are leaking?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Common signs: rooms that won&#x27;t reach temperature, vents with weak airflow, energy bills that keep rising while the equipment seems fine, excessive dust, and an attic or crawlspace that feels conditioned. A meaningful share of conditioned air in a typical home leaks out of the ducts before it reaches the rooms. We can inspect and measure to confirm."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Is duct sealing worth it?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "For most homes with leaky ducts, yes — you&#x27;re currently paying to heat and cool your attic and crawlspace. Sealing the leaks means the air you condition actually reaches your rooms, which improves comfort and reduces how hard your equipment has to work. We&#x27;ll tell you honestly whether sealing will make a real difference in your specific home."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can you add ductwork for an addition or remodel?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes. We design and install new ductwork for additions, remodels, and new construction — sized and laid out properly so the new space is comfortable and the existing system isn&#x27;t overtaxed. Adding square footage without planning the ductwork is a common reason additions never feel right."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "My airflow is weak at every vent — what causes that?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Whole-home weak airflow can come from undersized or restrictive ductwork, inadequate return air, a dirty blower or filter, crushed main trunk lines, or poor original design. It&#x27;s a diagnosis, not a guess — we measure and inspect to find which of those it is, because the fix is different for each."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Will fixing my ducts lower my energy bills?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Often, yes. When ducts leak or are poorly designed, your equipment runs longer and harder to compensate — and you still aren&#x27;t comfortable. Sealing leaks and correcting airflow means the system does its job in less run time. The size of the savings depends on how bad the duct problems are to start with."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Do I need new ductwork or just repairs?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "It depends on the condition and design of what you have. Sometimes targeted sealing and a few run replacements solve the problem; sometimes the original design is the problem and reworking it is the real fix. We inspect first and give you an honest recommendation — we&#x27;re not going to sell you a full duct replacement you don&#x27;t need."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Do you offer free estimates on ductwork?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "We provide free estimates on ductwork installation and replacement projects. An airflow diagnosis or duct inspection may carry a diagnostic fee, which we&#x27;ll tell you about upfront. Call (951) 634-3233 for current rates and to schedule."
        }
      }
    ]
  }
  </script>
